        <h1>The Toronto Shawarma Scene</h1>
        <div class="post-meta">
          May 27, 2026 &middot; 4 min read
          <span class="tag" style="margin-top:0;">Idea</span>
        </div>
        <p>These places are awesome, and often run by such great people, often families that have built the business from scratch. I was in the west end at a place called Shadi Shawarma recently that we always used to go to after playing at Scarlett Woods in high school, a good 5–7 years ago. I walk in and the first thing the guy says is "I remember when you guys used to come in here after golf, how have you been".</p>
        <p>First, I look a lot different than I did then. Second, I'm from Cabbagetown and this place is at Bloor and Runnymede. It's not like I'm passing by all the time. I genuinely don't know how he remembered me, but he did and that was pretty cool.</p>
        <p>Every interaction matters to these folks. That's why these places are special and that's probably why we keep going back. Because you always feel welcomed there.</p>
